  "query_preview": "../Tuesday through Friday/...The active pattern \ncontinues through the week, as a low sits in the western Gulf \njust south of Kenai Peninsula and brings multiple shortwaves into \nthe area, keeping precipitation and elevated winds in the forecast\nwith each wave passing through. High pressure begins to build \nover BC and extending into the panhandle, as well as some ridging \nbuilding in the Gulf, however an upper level trough swinging \nthrough Tuesday night into Wednesday will allow a weak shortwave \nto develop still and keep precipitation in the forecast for the \nouter Gulf coast and northern panhandle. This will break apart \nWednesday quickly, mainly remaining offshore before it begins to \nshear apart with the building high pressure. \n\nThe next system moving into the Gulf Thursday remains a bit \nuncertain in terms of timing and area of impact, largely dependent\non this high pressure and how long it keeps over the eastern Gulf\nand panhandle before the next system moves in. Some guidance \nstill suggests a brief cooling and drying period with the \nWednesday shortwave remaining more offshore and into the NE Gulf \nand the Thursday system being kept back by the ridging that builds\nover the area, giving more potential for cooler temperatures \nahead of Thursday and dropping snow levels across the northern \npanhandle and coastal range further inland. However, more model \nguidance is leaning towards the warmer and wetter solution with \nless cooling Wednesday, with snow levels staying higher over the \npanhandle and more lasting southerly flow into the area with this \nshortwave pushing further inland. Snow levels even with this \nsolution drop enough to keep a chance of a rain snow mix Thursday \nmorning at sea level for Haines and Skagway. \n\nThere is even further uncertainty Thursday through the weekend on\nhow far precipitation will even make it into the panhandle -- \nnamely if the low will remain far offshore and result in drier \nweather, or if it will progress eastward enough to impact the \nnorthern panhandle and coastline Thursday. Overall though, \nprecipitation chances and temperatures will trend downward Friday \nand possibly continue into the weekend as high pressure keeps the \nsystems further offshore in the western Gulf or sliding southeast \ninto the Pacific.",
